{"records":[{"id":"ftrm9sc5m9","title":"Quality control of ER membrane proteins by the RNF185/Membralin ubiquitin ligase complex","description":"Raw data for each figure of the manuscript","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-07-21T13:50:29.663Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ftrm9sc5m9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ftrm9sc5m9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"van de Weijer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pedro","last_name":"Carvalho","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Protein Degradation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ttkmsp5hsz","title":"Lipid mediated–phase separation of AGO proteins on ER controls nascent peptide ubiquitination","description":"AGO/miRNA-mediated gene silencing and ubiquitin-mediated protein quality control represent two fundamental mechanisms that control proper gene expression. Here we unexpectedly discover that fly and human AGO proteins, key components in the miRNA pathway, undergo lipid-mediated phase separation and condense into RNP-granules on the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) membrane to control protein production. Phase separation on the ER is mediated by electrostatic interactions between a conserved lipid-binding motif within the AGOs and the lipid PI(4,5)P2. The ER-localized AGO condensates recruit the E3 ubiquitin ligase Ltn1 to catalyze nascent peptide ubiquitination and coordinate with the VCP-Ufd1-Npl4 complex to process unwanted protein products for proteasomal degradation. Collectively, our study provides insight into the understanding of post-transcription-translation coupling controlled by AGOs via lipid-mediated phase separation.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-02-21T12:06:39.649Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ttkmsp5hsz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ttkmsp5hsz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Gao","last_name":"Yajie","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhu","last_name":"Yuanxiang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Molecular Biology","Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3p7xn6y4mj","title":"Role of a holo-insertase complex in the biogenesis of biophysically diverse ER membrane proteins ","description":"Unprocessed data images for \"Role of a holo-insertase complex in the biogenesis of biophysically diverse ER membrane proteins\" by Page, Nguyen, Pleiner et al.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-25T07:41:48.043Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3p7xn6y4mj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3p7xn6y4mj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Katharine","last_name":"Page","orcid":"0000-0001-8904-1244"},{"first_name":"Vy","last_name":"Nguyen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tino","last_name":"Pleiner","orcid":"0000-0002-5104-0315"},{"first_name":"Rebecca","last_name":"Voorhees","orcid":"0000-0003-1640-2293"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/006w34k90","name":"Howard Hughes Medical Institute","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Biochemistry","Molecular Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"w99dh6rymx","title":"RNF126-mediated reubiquitination is required for proteasomal degradation of p97-extracted membrane proteins. Hu et al","description":"Valosin-containing protein (VCP)/p97 is an AAA-ATPase that extracts poly-ubiquitinated substrates from multimeric macromolecular complexes and biological membranes for proteasomal degradation. During p97-mediated extraction, the substrate is largely deubiquitinated as it is threaded through the p97 central pore. How p97-extracted substrates are targeted to the proteasome with few or no ubiquitins is unknown. Here, we report that p97-extracted membrane proteins undergo a second round of ubiquitination catalyzed by the cytosolic ubiquitin ligase RNF126. RNF126 interacts with transmembrane domain-specific chaperone BAG6, which captures p97-liberated substrates. RNF126 depletion in cells diminishes the ubiquitination of extracted membrane proteins, slows down their turnover, and dramatically stabilizes otherwise transient intermediates in the cytosol. We reconstitute the reubiquitination of a p97-extracted, misfolded multi-spanning membrane protein with purified factors. Our results demonstrate that p97-extracted substrates need to rapidly engage ubiquitin ligase/chaperone pairs that rebuild the ubiquitin signal for proteasome targeting to prevent harmful accumulation of unfolded intermediates. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-06-12T15:36:45.32Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/w99dh6rymx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/w99dh6rymx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zairong","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xianyan","last_name":"Hu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Linhan","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuancai","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jia","last_name":"Ji","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jiqiang","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Congcong","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yaoyang","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhiyuan","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vfd47jgr8t","title":"Tapasin assembly surveillance by the RNF185/MBRL ubiquitin ligase complex regulates MHC-I surface expression","description":"Raw western blot data for each manuscript figure","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-09-04T20:17:51.948Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vfd47jgr8t","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vfd47jgr8t"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"van de Weijer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pedro","last_name":"Carvalho","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Protein Degradation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bkwjctz23n","title":"Regulation of phosphoribosyl-linked serine ubiquitination by deubiquitinases DupA and DupB, Shin et al","description":"The family of bacterial SidE enzymes catalyzes non-canonical phosphoribosyl-linked (PR) serine ubiquitination and promotes infectivity of Legionella pneumophila, a pathogenic bacterium causing Legionnaires’ disease. Here we describe identification of two bacterial effectors (LaiE and LaiF) that actively reverse PR-ubiquitination during infection and are thus named DeUbiquitinases for PR-ubiquitination (DUPs; DupA/LaiE and DupB/LaiF). Structural analyses revealed that DupA and SidE ubiquitin ligases harbor a highly homologous catalytic phosphodiesterase (PDE) domain. However, unlike SidE ubiquitin ligases, DupA displays increased affinity to PR-ubiquitinated substrates, which dictates its catalytic activity allowing DupA to predominantly cleave PR-ubiquitin from substrates. Interfering with DupA-ubiquitin binding switches its activity toward SidE-type ligase. Given the high affinity of DupA to PR-ubiquitinated substrates, we next exploited a catalytically inactive DupA mutant to trap and subsequently identify more than 180 PR-ubiquitinated host proteins in Legionella-infected cells. Proteins involved in endoplasmic reticulum (ER) fragmentation and membrane recruitment to Legionella-containing vacuoles (LCV) emerged as major SidE targets. The global map of PR-ubiquitinated substrates uncovered by DupA-mediated trapping strategy provides critical insights into host-pathogen interactions during Legionella infection.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-10-31T15:44:44.544Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bkwjctz23n","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bkwjctz23n"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ivan","last_name":"Dikic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Donghyuk","last_name":"Shin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rukmini","last_name":"Mukherjee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yaobin","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexis","last_name":"Gonzalez","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Structural Biology","Endoplasmic Reticulum","Ubiquitylation","Legionella"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"h6rn55h86x","title":"The Human E3 ligase RNF185 is a regulator of the SARS-CoV-2 envelope protein. Zou et al","description":"Supplementary data sets related to The Human E3 ligase RNF185 is a regulator of the SARS-CoV-2 envelope protein by Zou et al.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-03-27T13:44:21.344Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/h6rn55h86x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/h6rn55h86x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mikolaj","last_name":"Slabicki","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"kdfvg2rvjn","title":"Targeting Neddylation Sensitizes Colorectal Cancer to Topoisomerase I Inhibitors by Inactivating the DCAF13-CRL4 Ubiquitin Ligase Complex","description":"the raw data of all replicates conducted for the project \"Targeting Neddylation Sensitizes Colorectal Cancer to Topoisomerase I Inhibitors by Inactivating the DCAF13-CRL4 Ubiquitin Ligase Complex\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-03-27T14:13:31.651Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/kdfvg2rvjn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/kdfvg2rvjn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yilun","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Topoisomerase","Colorectal Cancer","Ubiquitylation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8vrk8wtmd3","title":"TMUB1 promotes p97-mediated segregation of hydrophobic transmembrane domains from the endoplasmic reticulum membrane","description":"Membrane protein clients of endoplasmic reticulum (ER)-associated degradation must be retrotranslocated from the ER membrane by the AAA-ATPase p97 for proteasomal degradation. Before direct engagement with p97, client transmembrane domains (TMDs) that have partially or fully crossed the membrane must be constantly shielded to avoid non-native interactions. How client TMDs are seamlessly escorted from the membrane to p97 is unknown. Here, we identified ER-resident TMUB1 as a TMD-specific escortase. TMUB1 interacts with the TMD of clients within the membrane and holds ~10-14 residues of hydrophobic sequence that is exposed out of membrane, using its transmembrane and cytosolic regions, respectively. The ubiquitin-like domain of TMUB1 recruits p97, which can pull client TMDs from bound TMUB1 into the cytosol. Disruption of TMUB1 escortase activity dramatically stabilizes otherwise transient retrotranslocating intermediates in the ER membrane and impairs client retrotranslocation. Thus, TMUB1 promotes TMD segregation by safeguarding TMD movement from the membrane to p97.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-08-11T18:07:17.86Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8vrk8wtmd3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8vrk8wtmd3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zai-Rong","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Linhan","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jiqiang","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qingchen","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Man-Xi","last_name":"Ge","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jia","last_name":"Ji","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Di","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yaoyang","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yang","last_name":"Cao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhiyuan","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"c8sk4g4tg5","title":"STIM1 and Endoplasmic Reticulum-Plasma Membrane Contact Sites Oscillate  Independently of Calcium-Induced Calcium Release","description":"Calcium (Ca²⁺) release from intracellular stores, Ca²⁺ entry across the plasma membrane, and their coordination via store-operated Ca²⁺ entry (SOCE) are critical for receptor-activated Ca²⁺ oscillations. However, the precise mechanism of Ca²⁺ oscillations and whether their control loop resides at the plasma membrane or intracellularly remain unresolved. By examining the dynamics of stromal interaction molecule 1 (STIM1), an endoplasmic reticulum (ER)-localized Ca²⁺ sensor that activates the Orai1 channel on the plasma membrane for SOCE, in mast cells, we found that a significant proportion of cells exhibited STIM1 oscillations with the same periodicity as Ca²⁺ oscillations. These cortical oscillations, shared with ER-plasma membrane (ER-PM) contact sites proteins, were only detectable using total internal reflection fluorescence microscopy (TIRFM). Notably, STIM1 oscillations could occur independently of Ca²⁺ oscillations. Simultaneous imaging of cytoplasmic Ca²⁺ and ER Ca²⁺ with CEPIA1er revealed that receptor activation does not deplete ER Ca²⁺, whereas receptor activation without extracellular Ca²⁺ influx induces cyclic ER Ca²⁺ depletion. However, under such nonphysiological conditions, cyclic ER Ca²⁺ oscillations lead to sustained STIM1 recruitment, indicating that oscillatory Ca²⁺ release is neither necessary nor sufficient for STIM1 oscillations. Using optogenetic tools to manipulate ER-PM contact site dynamics, we found that persistent ER-PM contact sites reduced the amplitude of Ca²⁺ oscillations without alteration of oscillation frequency. Together, these findings suggest an active cortical mechanism governs the rapid dissociation of ER-PM contact sites, thereby control amplitude of oscillatory Ca²⁺ dynamics during receptor-induced Ca²⁺ oscillations.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-30T15:57:50.975Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/c8sk4g4tg5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/c8sk4g4tg5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ding","last_name":"Xiong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chee San","last_name":"Tong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Suet Yin Sarah","last_name":"Fung","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Samantha","last_name":"McClellan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yang","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jeffery","last_name":"Yong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Min","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":135956,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":119,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":135956,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":135392,"restricted":64,"embargoed":500},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2323},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1515},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":659},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":475},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":437},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":422},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":411},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":377},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":335},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":279}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":133316},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1903},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":188},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":134},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":77},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":57},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":41},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":978},{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":957},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":507},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":362}]}}